# Summary of the Bill (Starting with Sec. 71101)

## Subchapter A—Reducing Fraud and Improving Enrollment Processes

**Sec. 71101. Moratorium on Medicare Savings Program rule.**
Prohibits the HHS Secretary from implementing or enforcing the CMS final rule published September 21, 2023 ("Streamlining Medicaid; Medicare Savings Program Eligibility Determination and Enrollment," 88 Fed. Reg. 65230) with respect to five specified sections of 42 C.F.R. (406.21(c), 435.4, 435.601, 435.911, and 435.952) through September 30, 2034. Appropriates $1,000,000 in FY2026 to the CMS Administrator for implementation of this section and Sec. 71102.

**Sec. 71102. Moratorium on Medicaid/CHIP/Basic Health Program rule.**
Prohibits the HHS Secretary from implementing or enforcing the CMS final rule published April 2, 2024 ("Streamlining the Medicaid, CHIP, and Basic Health Program Application, Eligibility Determination, Enrollment, and Renewal Processes," 89 Fed. Reg. 22780) with respect to specified provisions in 42 C.F.R. parts 431, 435, 447, and 457, through September 30, 2034. This blocks the rule's streamlining of eligibility and enrollment processes for those programs.

**Sec. 71103. Reducing duplicate enrollment under Medicaid and CHIP.**
Requires States (beginning no later than January 1, 2027) to regularly obtain enrollee address information from reliable sources (e.g., USPS returned mail, the National Change of Address Database, managed care entities), and beginning October 1, 2029, to submit enrollee Social Security numbers and other data at least monthly to a new HHS Secretary-established system to prevent simultaneous enrollment in multiple States' Medicaid plans. Appropriates $10,000,000 (FY2026) to establish the system and $20,000,000 (FY2029) to maintain it. Applies parallel requirements to CHIP and requires managed care contracts to transmit address information to States beginning January 1, 2027.

**Sec. 71104. Ensuring deceased individuals do not remain enrolled.**
Beginning January 1, 2027, States must check the Death Master File at least quarterly to identify deceased Medicaid enrollees, disenroll them, and discontinue payments (except for services furnished before death). States must immediately re-enroll, retroactively, any individual erroneously disenrolled due to misidentification as deceased.

**Sec. 71105. Ensuring deceased providers do not remain enrolled.**
Beginning January 1, 2028, States must check the Death Master File during provider enrollment, re-enrollment, or revalidation, and at least quarterly thereafter, to determine whether enrolled Medicaid providers or suppliers are deceased.

**Sec. 71106. Payment reduction for erroneous excess payments.**
Amends the Medicaid error-rate waiver provision (section 1903(u)) to allow audits by States (at the Secretary's option), cap the Secretary's waiver of erroneous excess payments at amounts exceeding the 0.03 allowable error rate, and expand the definition of erroneous payments to include payments where insufficient information is available to confirm eligibility. These changes apply beginning fiscal year 2030.

**Sec. 71107. Eligibility redeterminations.**
Requires States to conduct Medicaid eligibility redeterminations every 6 months (instead of annually) for individuals enrolled under the ACA expansion population (section 1902(a)(10)(A)(i)(VIII)) or under equivalent expansion waivers, effective for redeterminations scheduled on or after the first quarter beginning after December 31, 2026. Directs HHS to issue implementation guidance within 180 days of enactment and appropriates $75,000,000 in FY2026 to CMS for implementation.

**Sec. 71108. Revising home equity limit for long-term care eligibility.**
Caps the Medicaid home equity interest limit at $1,000,000 (previously $500,000 baseline, inflation-adjusted), while allowing States to apply the lower limit to homes on agriculturally zoned lots and to elect a limit up to $1,000,000 for other homes. Clarifies that the home equity limit must be applied in determining eligibility for nursing facility and long-term care services. Effective January 1, 2028.

**Sec. 71109. Alien Medicaid eligibility.**
Beginning October 1, 2026, restricts federal Medicaid payments to States to individuals who are U.S. citizens or nationals, lawfully admitted permanent residents, Cuban and Haitian entrants, or citizens of Compact of Free Association nations—thereby excluding other lawfully present immigrants (such as asylees and refugees) who were previously eligible. Applies the same restriction to CHIP (except for emergency services under section 2105(a)(1)(D)(ii)) and appropriates $15,000,000 in FY2026 to CMS for implementation.

**Sec. 71110. Expansion FMAP for emergency Medicaid.**
Beginning October 1, 2026, caps the federal medical assistance percentage (FMAP) for emergency medical services furnished to the aliens described in section 1903(v)(1) at the State's regular FMAP under section 1905(b), eliminating the enhanced 90% expansion FMAP for such emergency services. Appropriates $1,000,000 in FY2026 to CMS for implementation.

## Subchapter B—Preventing Wasteful Spending

**Sec. 71111. Moratorium on long-term care staffing rule.**
Prohibits the HHS Secretary from implementing or enforcing the CMS final rule published May 10, 2024 ("Minimum Staffing Standards for Long-Term Care Facilities and Medicaid Institutional Payment Transparency Reporting," 89 Fed. Reg. 40876) with respect to 42 C.F.R. sections 483.5 and 483.35, through September 30, 2034. This blocks the rule's minimum nurse staffing requirements for nursing facilities.

**Sec. 71112. Reducing State Medicaid costs.**
Shortens Medicaid retroactive coverage from 3 months to 1 month before application for expansion adults (section 1902(a)(10)(A)(i)(VIII)) and to 2 months for all other individuals, effective for applications made on or after the first quarter beginning after December 31, 2026. Limits CHIP and pregnancy-related retroactive coverage to no earlier than the second month before application. Appropriates $10,000,000 in FY2026 to CMS for implementation.

**Sec. 71113. Federal payments to prohibited entities.**
Prohibits the use of federal Medicaid direct spending to make payments to a "prohibited entity"—defined as a 501(c)(3) tax-exempt essential community provider primarily engaged in family planning/reproductive health that provides abortions beyond rape, incest, or life-endangerment exceptions, and that received over $800,000 in combined federal and state Medicaid expenditures in FY2023 (directly or through managed care entities)—for items and services furnished during the 1-year period beginning on enactment. This provision effectively defunds Planned Parenthood from Medicaid for one year. Appropriates $1,000,000 in FY2026 to CMS for implementation.

## Subchapter C—Stopping Abusive Financing Practices

**Sec. 71114. Sunsetting increased FMAP incentive.**
Conditions the enhanced 90% FMAP for ACA expansion States on the State having begun expending amounts for expansion individuals prior to January 1, 2026. States that begin expansion coverage on or after that date will not receive the enhanced FMAP.

**Sec. 71115. Provider taxes.**
Beginning October 1, 2026, gradually lowers the provider tax hold-harmless threshold for expansion States from 6% to 5.5% (FY2028), 5% (FY2029), 4.5% (FY2030), 4% (FY2031), and 3.5% (FY2032 and thereafter), while freezing existing thresholds for non-expansion States and for certain taxes on classes described in 42 C.F.R. 433.56(a)(3)–(4) already in effect in expansion States. Applies only to the 50 States and D.C. (not territories) and appropriates $20,000,000 in FY2026 to CMS for implementation.

**Sec. 71116. State directed payments.**
Limits Medicaid managed care state directed payment rates to 100% of Medicare rates for expansion States and 110% for non-expansion States for rating periods beginning on or after enactment. Payments approved before May 1, 2025 (or for rural hospitals approved by enactment) are grandfathered but reduced by 10 percentage points annually beginning January 1, 2028 until they reach the applicable cap. Appropriates $7,000,000 annually for FY2026–2033 to CMS for implementation.

**Sec. 71117. Waiver of uniform tax requirement for provider taxes.**
Tightens the rules for when a Medicaid provider tax qualifies for a waiver of the uniformity requirement, providing that a tax is "generally redistributive" (and thus ineligible for waiver) if it imposes lower rates on taxpayers with fewer Medicaid taxable units, higher rates based on Medicaid versus non-Medicaid units, or uses terminology that achieves the same effect without explicitly referencing Medicaid. Effective on enactment, subject to a Secretary-determined transition period of up to 3 fiscal years, and applies only to the 50 States and D.C.

**Sec. 71118. Budget neutrality for section 1115 demonstrations.**
Beginning January 1, 2027, the HHS Secretary may not approve, renew, or amend a Medicaid section 1115 demonstration project unless the CMS Chief Actuary certifies it will not increase federal expenditures relative to what would be spent absent the project. Requires the Secretary to specify a methodology for carrying forward savings into subsequent approval periods. Appropriates $5,000,000 for each of FY2026 and FY2027 to CMS for implementation.

## Subchapter D—Increasing Personal Accountability

**Sec. 71119. Community engagement (work) requirements.**
Beginning no later than the first quarter after December 31, 2026, requires States to condition Medicaid eligibility for "applicable individuals" (expansion adults aged 19–64 who are not pregnant, disabled, or Medicare-entitled) on demonstrating 80 hours per month of work, community service, work program participation, education, or equivalent income. Exempts specified populations including parents/caregivers of children 13 and under, Indians, veterans rated totally disabled, medically frail individuals, inmates, and pregnant/postpartum individuals; allows hardship exemptions (e.g., hospitalization, declared disasters, counties with unemployment at or above 8% or 1.5 times the national rate). Noncompliant individuals get a 30-day cure period before disenrollment; States may not waive the requirement under section 1115, and the Secretary may grant good-faith implementation exemptions only through December 31, 2028. Appropriates $100,000,000 (formula grants), $100,000,000 (equal distribution), and $200,000,000 (CMS implementation) in FY2026, and requires an interim final rule by June 1, 2026.

**Sec. 71120. Cost sharing for certain expansion individuals.**
Beginning October 1, 2028, requires States to impose cost sharing (determined by the State, greater than $0) on Medicaid expansion individuals with family income above the federal poverty line, while prohibiting premiums or enrollment fees for them. Cost sharing is capped at $35 per item or service (with prescription drug limits mirroring section 1916A), excluded for primary care, mental health, substance use disorder, FQHC, CCBHC, and rural health clinic services, and capped in aggregate at 5% of quarterly or monthly family income. Providers may condition care on payment, and appropriates $15,000,000 in FY2026 to CMS for implementation.

## Subchapter E—Expanding Access to Care

**Sec. 71121. Home or community-based services (HCBS) adjustments.**
Beginning July 1, 2028, allows the Secretary to approve standalone section 1915(c) waivers (initial 3-year term, renewable for 5-year periods) providing HCBS to individuals who do not require an institutional level-of-care determination, subject to State needs-based criteria, demonstration that the waiver won't increase wait times for existing waiver participants, and per-capita cost limits. Appropriates $50,000,000 (FY2026) for implementation and $100,000,000 (FY2027) for State HCBS delivery systems, distributed based on each State's share of the national HCBS population.

## Chapter 2—Medicare

**Sec. 71201. Limiting Medicare coverage of certain individuals.**
Restricts Medicare eligibility to U.S. citizens or nationals, lawfully admitted permanent residents, Cuban and Haitian entrants, and Compact of Free Association residents—excluding other lawfully present aliens. For individuals already enrolled at enactment, the restriction applies 18 months after enactment, and the Commissioner of Social Security must complete a review within 1 year identifying and notifying affected individuals of termination.

**Sec. 71202. Temporary Medicare physician fee schedule increase.**
Extends the temporary 2.5% payment increase under the Medicare physician fee schedule for exceptional circumstances to services furnished between January 1, 2026 and January 1, 2027, conforming related budget-neutrality provisions in section 1848.

**Sec. 71203. Orphan drug exclusion under Drug Price Negotiation Program.**
Expands the Medicare Drug Price Negotiation Program's orphan drug exclusion to cover drugs designated for one or more rare diseases or conditions (previously limited to a single rare disease), and clarifies that a drug retains its "orphan" exclusion status until the first day it no longer qualifies as such. Applies to initial price applicability years beginning on or after January 1, 2028.

## Chapter 3—Health Tax

**Sec. 71301. Premium tax credit eligibility restrictions.**
Limits the ACA premium tax credit to individuals who are lawfully present and are "eligible aliens"—defined as lawfully admitted permanent residents, Cuban and Haitian entrants, or Compact of Free Association residents—excluding other lawfully present immigrants (such as asylees, refugees, and those with temporary protected status). Requires exchanges to verify eligible alien status and applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026.

**Sec. 71302. Disallowing premium tax credit during Medicaid ineligibility due to alien status.**
Repeals section 36B(c)(1)(B) of the Internal Revenue Code, which allowed lawfully present immigrants ineligible for Medicaid due to their immigration status to qualify for premium tax credits. Applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71303. Verification of premium tax credit eligibility.**
Requires exchanges to verify an applicant's eligibility (household income, family size, eligible alien status, coverage status, and residence) before any month counts as a "coverage month" for premium tax credit purposes, and requires exchanges to offer a pre-enrollment verification process beginning no later than August 1 each year. The Secretary may waive verification for special enrollment periods based on family size changes. Applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2027.

**Sec. 71304. Disallowing premium tax credit for certain special enrollment period coverage.**
Excludes from "coverage months" any plan enrolled in during a special enrollment period based solely on projected household income relative to the poverty line (rather than a qualifying life event), making such coverage ineligible for premium tax credits. Applies to plan years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71305. Eliminating limitation on recapture of advance premium tax credits.**
Repeals the statutory cap (section 36B(f)(2)(B)) limiting the amount of excess advance premium tax credits that must be repaid by taxpayers with household income below 400% of the federal poverty line, requiring full repayment of any excess advance credits. Applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71306. Permanent telehealth safe harbor for HDHPs.**
Makes permanent the safe harbor allowing high deductible health plans to cover telehealth and other remote care services without a deductible (or below the minimum deductible) without disqualifying HSA eligibility. Applies to plan years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 71307. Bronze and catastrophic plans with HSAs.**
Allows individuals enrolled in ACA Exchange bronze or catastrophic plans (under section 1302(d)(1)(A) or (e) of the ACA) to contribute to health savings accounts by treating such plans as high deductible health plans. Applies to months beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71308. Direct primary care arrangements.**
Provides that direct primary care service arrangements (fixed periodic fees for primary care services, capped at $150/month, or $300 for arrangements covering more than one individual) do not disqualify individuals from HSA eligibility, and treats such fees as qualified medical expenses payable from HSAs. Excludes procedures requiring general anesthesia, prescription drugs (other than vaccines), and non-ambulatory laboratory services from the definition of primary care services. Applies to months beginning after December 31, 2025, with the $150 cap inflation-adjusted from calendar year 2025.

## Chapter 4—Protecting Rural Hospitals and Providers

**Sec. 71401. Rural Health Transformation Program.**
Appropriates $50,000,000,000 total ($10,000,000,000 annually for FY2026–2030) to CMS for allotments to the 50 States that submit approved rural health transformation plans by December 31, 2025, with 50% distributed equally among approved States and 50% allocated based on rural population, rural health facility counts, and hospital situation factors. States must use funds for at least 3 of 10 specified activities (e.g., chronic disease prevention, technology adoption including AI and remote monitoring, rural clinician recruitment with 5-year service commitments, opioid/substance use treatment access), with no matching funds required and no more than 10% for administration. Unexpended funds return to the Treasury after October 1, 2032, and there is no administrative or judicial review of allotment decisions. Appropriates an additional $200,000,000 in FY2025 for implementation.

## Subtitle C—Increase in Debt Limit

**Sec. 72001. Modification of limitation on the public debt.**
Increases the statutory limit on the public debt under 31 U.S.C. 3101(b) by $5,000,000,000,000 (from its level set by section 401(b) of Public Law 118-5).

## Subtitle D—Unemployment

**Sec. 73001. Ending unemployment payments to jobless millionaires.**
Prohibits the use of federal funds to pay unemployment compensation benefits to individuals whose base-period wages equal or exceed $1,000,000, effective for weeks of unemployment beginning on enactment. Requires self-certification of wage eligibility on applications, directs State agencies to verify wage eligibility using available systems, and requires recovery of overpayments. Covers federal civilian employee and ex-servicemember unemployment programs, extended benefits, and other federal unemployment programs as determined by the Secretary of Labor. [end of text]


